NUM-48: apply first-order decay as exp(-K1 dt), and growth under STEADY

getReactedQual(), used for conduits and storage units under KINWAVE and
DYNWAVE, computed c * (1 - K1 dt), the explicit Euler step, instead of
c * exp(-K1 dt) of reference manual eq. 5-6. The result depended on the
routing step (a pond decaying for 6 h at K1 = 24/day ends 0.8 %, 4.9 % and
23 % low with 10, 60 and 300 s steps) and K1 dt >= 1 removed everything.
Use exp(), which also handles a negative K1 (growth) exactly. In
findSFLinkQual() (STEADY), which already uses exp(), apply the reaction for
any non-zero K1 instead of only K1 > 0, so growth is not ignored there.

Apply from the root of a Stormwater-Management-Model checkout at cb3e192b:
    git apply NUM-48_swmm530.patch

diff --git a/src/legacy/engine/qualrout.c b/src/legacy/engine/qualrout.c
index 1cf5ae1..7afd410 100644
--- a/src/legacy/engine/qualrout.c
+++ b/src/legacy/engine/qualrout.c
@@ -433,7 +433,7 @@ void findSFLinkQual(int i, double qSeep, double fEvap, double tStep)
 
         // --- apply first-order decay over travel time
         c2 = c1;
-        if (Pollut[p].kDecay > 0.0)
+        if (Pollut[p].kDecay != 0.0)
         {
             c2 = c1 * exp(-Pollut[p].kDecay * tStep);
             c2 = MAX(0.0, c2);
@@ -610,8 +610,7 @@ double getReactedQual(int p, double c, double v1, double tStep)
 
     if (kDecay == 0.0)
         return c;
-    c2 = c * (1.0 - kDecay * tStep);
-    c2 = MAX(0.0, c2);
+    c2 = c * exp(-kDecay * tStep);
     lossRate = (c - c2) * v1 / tStep;
     massbal_addReactedMass(p, lossRate);
     return c2;
